Semi-anaerobic fermentation process for ethanol production;
It is commonly used for ethanol production usually involving yeasts such as Saccharomyces cerevisiae.
The procedure is as follows;
1.Preparation of the fermentation medium;
Ingredients;
Prepare a medium rich in nutrients containing.
Carbon source;
glucose, sucrose or starch which is from 20 to 25% w/v.
Nitrogen source;
ammonium sulfate or urea.
Minerals;
phosphates and trace elements.
Adjust pH to 4.5-5.0.Sterilize the medium at 121°C for 15-20 minutes.
2.Inoculation;
Inoculate the sterilized medium with an active yeast culture.Use sufficient inoculum 5 to 10% v/v to ensure rapid initiation of fermentation.
3.Semi-anaerobic conditions;
Maintain semi-anaerobic conditions by providing a limited amount of oxygen.Use a shake flask or bioreactor with reduced agitation.Ensure that the oxygen supply is minimal to favor ethanol production over biomass growth.Avoid complete anaerobic conditions to avoid metabolic stress on the yeast.
4.Fermentation Process;
Temperature;
Maintain temperature at 30 to 35°C optimal for yeast activity.
Duration;Allow fermentation for 48 to 72 hours.
Monitoring;Monitor sugar consumption and ethanol production with tests such as HPLC or refractometry.
5.Harvesting Ethanol;
At the end of fermentation separate the yeast cells from the fermented liquid by centrifugation or filtration.Distill the fermented liquor to recover the ethanol.Purify the ethanol by fractional distillation if higher purity is required.

